WEBVTT

00:00:00.010 --> 00:00:12.250
<v Ira Glass>There's this political parable about Hillary Clinton that made the rounds during the primaries this year, a parable with many layers to it. One of our contributors, Jack Hitt, wrote about this recently for the New Republic.
<v Ira Glass>今年初选期间，政界流传着一则关于希拉里·克林顿的寓言，这则寓言有着多重含义。我们的撰稿人之一杰克·希特（Jack Hitt）最近在《新共和》杂志上对此进行了报道。

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:18.100
<v Jack Hitt>Elizabeth Warren is the person who tells this story. So this goes all the way back to the late 90s when Bill Clinton was still in office.
<v Jack Hitt>伊丽莎白·沃伦（Elizabeth Warren）是讲这个故事的人。故事要追溯到90年代末，当时比尔·克林顿还在任。

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:23.280
<v Ira Glass>Hillary Clinton was First Lady. Elizabeth Warren wasn't a senator yet. She was a law professor at Harvard.
<v Ira Glass>希拉里·克林顿当时是第一夫人。伊丽莎白·沃伦还不是参议员，她是哈佛大学的法学教授。

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:38.710
<v Jack Hitt>And she had written, I think, an op-ed piece about this one bankruptcy bill. And Warren tells the story of the First Lady making contact. And they sat down. And she said, it was just amazing talking to Hillary Clinton.
<v Jack Hitt>我想当时她写了一篇关于某项破产法案的评论文章。沃伦讲述了第一夫人与她联系的故事。她们坐下来谈了谈。她说，与希拉里·克林顿交谈真是太棒了。

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:43.350
<v Elizabeth Warren>And just the two of us. They closed the door. Mrs. Clinton sits down. We have hamburgers and french fries.
<v Elizabeth Warren>当时只有我们两个人。她们关上了门。克林顿夫人坐了下来。我们吃了汉堡包和炸薯条。

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:44.360
<v Bill Moyer>You tutor her.
<v Bill Moyer>你辅导了她。

00:00:44.360 --> 00:01:00.310
<v Ira Glass>This is Elizabeth Warren telling the story to Bill Moyers on television in 2004. One of the problems with this bill, she explained, is that if a man went bankrupt, he would have to pay off his credit card companies and his banks before he would pay off an ex-wife or child support and alimony. She explained this to Moyers.
<v Ira Glass>这是伊丽莎白·沃伦2004年在电视上对比尔·莫耶斯（Bill Moyers）讲述的故事。她解释说，该法案的问题之一是，如果一个人破产了，他必须先偿还信用卡公司和银行的钱，然后才能支付前妻的赡养费或子女抚养费。她向莫耶斯解释了这一点。

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:20.790
<v Elizabeth Warren>And I got to tell you, I never had a smarter student. Quick, right to the heart of it. I go over the law. It's a complex law. Went over the economics, showed her the graphs, showed her the charts. And she got it. Within 20 minutes, she could play where the rest of it would come. Well then, that will mean this part's happened. That will mean this is happening.
<v Elizabeth Warren>我得告诉你，我从未教过比她更聪明的学生。反应很快，直击要害。我讲解了这项法律。这是一部复杂的法律。我讲了经济学，给她看了图表，给她看了数据。她全懂了。20分钟内，她就能推导出后续的影响。好吧，这意味着这部分会发生。这意味着那部分正在发生。

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:29.140
<v Jack Hitt>And she got right to the heart of it, totally understood it, and went back and convinced Bill to veto this bankruptcy bill.
<v Jack Hitt>她直击要害，完全理解了，并回去说服比尔否决了这项破产法案。

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:34.100
<v Ira Glass>Warren said Bill Clinton had been planning on signing the bill. She credits Hillary with turning him around.
<v Ira Glass>沃伦说比尔·克林顿本来打算签署该法案。她将他的回心转意归功于希拉里。

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:50.880
<v Jack Hitt>And that's the Hillary that we all recognize, if you've read any of the magazine bios of her that go all the way back to college, the valedictorian, the A-plus student, the woman who does her homework, figures it out, and has the best understanding. But then, Warren tells a second part to the story.
<v Jack Hitt>如果你读过任何关于她的杂志传记，从大学时的告别演说致辞人、全A学生、那个做作业、钻研问题并拥有最深刻理解力的女人开始，这就是我们大家都认识的希拉里。但随后，沃伦又讲了故事的第二部分。

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:57.510
<v Ira Glass>This part happens just a few months after the bill is vetoed. It's 2001, Clinton is no longer the First Lady.
<v Ira Glass>这部分发生在法案被否决几个月后。那是2001年，克林顿已经不再是第一夫人了。

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:06.630
<v Jack Hitt>So this time, she's a United States senator. She's been elected. A lot of her campaign money came from Wall Street, as Warren points out when she tells this story.
<v Jack Hitt>所以这一次，她是美国参议员了。她当选了。正如沃伦在讲这个故事时所指出的，她的竞选资金很大一部分来自华尔街。

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:19.530
<v Elizabeth Warren>And one of the first bills that came up after she was Senator Clinton was the bankruptcy bill. This is a bill that's like a vampire. It will not die. There's a lot of money behind it. And it--
<v Elizabeth Warren>希拉里当选参议员后提出的首批法案之一就是破产法案。这是一个像吸血鬼一样的法案。它怎么也死不了。背后有大量的资金。而且它……

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:21.680
<v Bill Moyer>And Bill, her husband, had vetoed.
<v Bill Moyer>而她的丈夫比尔曾否决过它。

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:24.780
<v Elizabeth Warren>Her husband had vetoed it very much at her urging.
<v Elizabeth Warren>她的丈夫正是在她的极力催促下否决了它。

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:25.740
<v Bill Moyer>And?
<v Bill Moyer>然后呢？

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.900
<v Elizabeth Warren>She voted in favor of it.
<v Elizabeth Warren>她投票支持它。

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:40.150
<v Jack Hitt>And of course, that's the Hillary that is often described as the opportunist, the woman who will do anything to keep power.
<v Jack Hitt>当然，这就是那个常被描述为机会主义者的希拉里，那个为了保住权力不惜一切代价的女人。

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:41.020
<v Ira Glass>The schemer.
<v Ira Glass>阴谋家。

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:43.480
<v Jack Hitt>Yeah, the captive of Wall Street.
<v Jack Hitt>是的，华尔街的俘虏。

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:54.390
<v Ira Glass>But then, there's yet another turn to the story. And that's in Hillary Clinton's version of the story. Hillary Clinton says, yes. She voted for the bill. But she did not do that to suck up to Wall Street. She says--
<v Ira Glass>但随后，故事又有了另一个转折。那是希拉里·克林顿的版本。希拉里·克林顿说，是的。她投了赞成票。但她这样做不是为了讨好华尔街。她说……

00:02:54.390 --> 00:03:01.480
<v Jack Hitt>She had quietly lobbied to insert language that would protect the vulnerable populations that Elizabeth Warren had warned her about, like single moms.
<v Jack Hitt>她曾私下游说加入保护弱势群体的措辞，这些群体正是伊丽莎白·沃伦警告过她的，比如单身母亲。

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:01.810
<v Hillary Clinton>I took that on. And I went to the floor. And I buttonholed the Democrats and the Republicans who were leading that bill. I said, you can't do this. You--
<v Hillary Clinton>我承担了这项工作。我来到议会大厅。我私下说服了领导该法案的民主党人和共和党人。我说，你们不能这样做。你们……

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:35.370
<v Jack Hitt>And so, when you hear this version of the story, then you see, oh yes. Right. Well, this is the Hillary Clinton that many liberal commentators love to talk about, the really smart, sharp, the legislator, the one who understands that so many of these legal battles, these legislative battles, happen inside the parentheses of some footnote somewhere, a detail-oriented, shrewd--
<v Jack Hitt>所以，当你听到这个版本的故事情节时，你会想，哦，是的。对。好吧，这就是许多自由派评论员喜欢谈论的希拉里·克林顿，那个非常聪明、敏锐的立法者，那个明白许多法律斗争、立法斗争都发生在某个脚注括号里的细节导向型、精明的……

00:03:35.370 --> 00:04:03.350
<v Ira Glass>In this version of the story, Hillary gets to have her cake and eat it too. When she votes for the bill, it pleases Wall Street. When she adds the provision for divorced moms, it pleases her liberal base. Everybody's happy. But there is yet one last turn to the screw. In 2005, the bill comes up yet again in the Senate. It still has not become law. And this time, the protections that Clinton had fought for, for mothers and other vulnerable populations, those were not in the bill.
<v Ira Glass>在这个故事版本中，希拉里鱼与熊掌兼得。当她为法案投票时，她讨好了华尔街。当她为离婚母亲增加条款时，她讨好了她的自由派基础。每个人都很高兴。但故事还有最后一个转折。2005年，该法案再次提交参议院。它仍然没有成为法律。而这一次，克林顿曾为母亲和其他弱势群体争取的保护条款，都没有出现在法案中。

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:14.010
<v Jack Hitt>So there's no way out for her on this one. She has to declare her side, who she's really defending in Congress, Wall Street or these vulnerable populations that elect her to office.
<v Jack Hitt>所以她这次没路可退了。她必须表明立场，她在国会真正捍卫的是谁，是华尔街，还是选她上台的那些弱势群体。

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:15.200
<v Ira Glass>Mm-hm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:31.520
<v Jack Hitt>And what happens? Bill is in the hospital for something. And she goes to his bedside and claims that she can't be present for the vote in Congress. Essentially she goes AWOL from the decision and is not there to vote either way.
<v Jack Hitt>结果怎么样？比尔因为某些原因住院了。她去床边探望他，声称自己无法出席国会的投票。本质上，她从决策中脱岗了，没去投票。

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:37.440
<v Ira Glass>She does issue a statement saying that she's against the bill. But she doesn't actually vote against the bill. It passes.
<v Ira Glass>她确实发表声明说她反对该法案。但她实际上并没有对法案投反对票。它通过了。

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:50.230
<v Jack Hitt>And this is the Hillary that drives a lot of people insane. Because this one seems even more Machiavellian, whatever it takes to save my skin.
<v Jack Hitt>这就是让很多人抓狂的希拉里。因为这看起来甚至更马基雅维利，为了保全自己不择手段。

00:04:50.230 --> 00:05:02.270
<v Ira Glass>In his article, Jack had list the different Hillarys that we see in different stories about her. There's the A-student, the opportunist. There's the mastermind, the rat-fink, the pragmatist, the truth-twister.
<v Ira Glass>在文章中，杰克列出了我们在不同故事中看到的不同的希拉里。有全A学生，机会主义者。有策划者、告密者、务实者、歪曲事实者。

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:15.910
<v Jack Hitt>Almost any story you hear about Hillary, you can kind of break down these various Hillarys in that story. We run across her in all of these variations all the time.
<v Jack Hitt>你听到的几乎任何关于希拉里的故事，你都可以在其中分解出这些不同的希拉里。我们一直都在这些变体中遇见她。

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:38.180
<v Ira Glass>I have to say, one of the things with Hillary is that she can seem like all of these Hillarys at once sometimes. And then, occasionally, you get a glimpse of a side of Hillary Clinton that is none of those, that doesn't fit any of the regular pictures we have of her. We have a story like that for you today. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>我不得不说，希拉里的一点是，她有时看起来像所有这些希拉里的集合体。然后，偶尔，你会瞥见希拉里·克林顿不属于其中任何一种的一面，这一面不符合我们对她的任何常规描述。今天我们有一个这样的故事要讲给你听。来自芝加哥WBEZ，这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:56.880
<v Ira Glass>Here's what we're going to do on today's program. We have this story about Hillary Clinton that, when I saw it, I feel like it gave me a picture of her that-- first of all, it comes from completely credible sources, FBI interviews, in fact. And it painted a very different picture from what we have all been hearing from both sides in this election.
<v Ira Glass>今天节目我们要讲的是：我们有一个关于希拉里·克林顿的故事，当我看到它时，我觉得它给了我一张她的肖像，首先，它来自完全可靠的消息来源，实际上是联邦调查局（FBI）的访谈记录。它描绘的画面与我们在这次选举中双方听到的完全不同。

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:18.970
<v Ira Glass>And then, after that story, all of us here at our show all thought, OK, everybody is so tired of this election and so stressed out on the left and the right-- like, everyone is so freaked out. What's going to happen if their candidate loses? We thought, here at our show, let's just have something funny on the radio and on the internet. So we're going to have that too. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>在那之后，我们节目组的所有人都想，好吧，大家对这次选举已经非常厌倦，左派和右派都倍感压力——每个人都快崩溃了。如果他们的候选人输了会怎样？我们想，不如在广播和互联网上放点有趣的东西。所以我们也要这么做。留下来。

00:06:18.970 --> 00:06:52.390
<v Ira Glass>Serve or Be Served-- so Hillary Clinton's polling numbers took a dive this week, of course, after FBI Director James Comey said agents would be reviewing more of her e-mails. There are two basic stories out there about Hillary Clinton's e-mails and her decision to use a private server for her e-mails when she was Secretary of State. The Clinton version of what happened is that she made a mistake. There's nothing in those e-mails. This is her enemies making a lot of noise about nothing.
<v Ira Glass>服务还是被服务——当然，在FBI局长詹姆斯·科米（James Comey）表示特工将审查更多她的电子邮件后，希拉里·克林顿的民调数据本周暴跌。关于希拉里·克林顿的电子邮件以及她担任国务卿时决定使用私人服务器处理邮件，外界主要有两种说法。克林顿的版本是她犯了一个错误。那些邮件里什么都没有。这是她的敌人在无事生非。

00:06:52.390 --> 00:07:11.450
<v Ira Glass>The Trump version of what happened is that she's a criminal and that she will be prosecuted, she will serve time over those e-mails and the way she's handled them. This was her using a private server to keep secrets from the American people. But I was really struck a few weeks ago reading this article on Politico. If you don't know Politico, it's a nonpartisan political news site and magazine.
<v Ira Glass>特朗普的版本是她是个罪犯，她会因为这些邮件以及她处理邮件的方式而被起诉、坐牢。这是她使用私人服务器向美国人民隐瞒秘密。但我几周前读到《政治报》（Politico）上的这篇文章时，真的深受震撼。如果你不知道《政治报》，它是一个无党派的政治新闻网站和杂志。

00:07:11.450 --> 00:07:36.340
<v Ira Glass>And this article told a history of the e-mails that, for all the commotion about these e-mails over this past year, I had never heard this. What they did was simple. After the FBI released its summaries of the interviews that its investigators did with Hillary Clinton and State Department officials and intel experts and CIA experts and some of Clinton's closest aides, a political writer named Garrett Graff read through the 247 pages of summaries.
<v Ira Glass>这篇文章讲述了这些电子邮件的历史，尽管过去一年来围绕这些邮件闹得沸沸扬扬，但我从未听说过。他们做的事情很简单。在FBI发布了调查人员对希拉里·克林顿、国务院官员、情报专家、中央情报局专家以及克林顿一些核心助手进行访谈的总结后，一位名叫加勒特·格拉夫（Garrett Graff）的政治作家通读了这247页的摘要。

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:52.200
<v Ira Glass>It's a public document. You can get a copy online yourself. But I think maybe only a handful of reporters have actually read through the entirety of it. And then, Garrett Graff put together an exhaustive and, frankly, very page-turney account of exactly what happened, according to what the FBI found.
<v Ira Glass>这是一份公开文件。你自己可以在网上查到副本。但我认为可能只有少数记者真正通读了全部内容。然后，加勒特·格拉夫拼凑出一份详尽的，坦率地说，非常引人入胜的叙述，详细记录了FBI发现的究竟发生了什么。

00:07:52.200 --> 00:08:10.230
<v Ira Glass>That is, how Hillary Clinton and her aides ended up doing her e-mails this way. What were they thinking? How did this happen? How did she end up running her e-mail from a private server in the basement of her house, including e-mails with classified information on them? Garrett Graff talk to one of our producers, Sean Cole, about this. Here's Sean.
<v Ira Glass>也就是说，希拉里·克林顿和她的助手最终是如何以这种方式处理她的电子邮件的。他们在想什么？这是怎么发生的？她是怎么最终在自家地下室用私人服务器处理电子邮件的，包括那些带有机密信息的邮件？加勒特·格拉夫与我们的一位制片人肖恩·科尔（Sean Cole）谈论了这个问题。这是肖恩。

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:25.280
<v Sean Cole>Garrett Graff spent three days reading the interview summaries. He's been covering the FBI for almost a decade. And he's pored over a lot of documents like these. And his conclusion, after reading the 247 pages, is this. The way Hillary and her top aides dealt with her e-mails is indeed scandalous. It's just a totally different scandal than people thought.
<v Sean Cole>加勒特·格拉夫花了三天时间阅读访谈摘要。他报道FBI新闻近十年了。他查阅过很多这样的文件。读完这247页后，他的结论是：希拉里和她的高层助手处理邮件的方式确实丑闻缠身。只是这与人们想象的完全是另一回事。

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:59.720
<v Garrett Graff>I think a lot of us thought that this was done out of sophistication. That it was done out of this very advanced Machiavellian understanding of federal records laws and advanced knowledge of computer technology. But by the time the FBI got done with all of these interviews, it becomes really clear it was actually done out of technological ignorance.
<v Garrett Graff>我想我们很多人都认为这是出于老练。这是出于对联邦记录法律的极高明、马基雅维利式的理解，以及对计算机技术的先进知识。但当FBI完成所有这些访谈时，很明显，这实际上是出于技术无知。

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:29.150
<v Sean Cole>Garrett writes that the documents, quote, "depict less a sinister and carefully calculated effort to avoid transparency than a busy and uninterested executive who shows little comfort with even the basics of technology, working with a small, harried, inner circle of aides. Reading the FBI interviews," he writes," Clinton's team hardly seems organized enough to mount any sort of sinister cover up."
<v Sean Cole>加勒特写道，这些文件“描述的不是一种阴险且精心策划的逃避透明度的企图，而是一个忙碌且对技术并不感兴趣的高管，她表现得对基本技术都不太适应，身边只有一小群惊慌失措的核心助手。阅读FBI的访谈，”他写道，“克林顿的团队似乎组织性太差，无法进行任何形式的阴险掩盖。”

00:09:29.150 --> 00:09:50.790
<v Sean Cole>So beginning at the beginning. When Hillary took over Secretary of State, she and her staff had to figure out how she would handle e-mail. Hillary had always checked her e-mail on a BlackBerry. And she requested a secure government-issued BlackBerry. But she was told she wouldn't be able to use her personal e-mail account on it. That wasn't allowed. And using government e-mail for personal stuff was discouraged.
<v Sean Cole>让我们从头说起。当希拉里接任国务卿时，她和她的员工必须找出她该如何处理电子邮件。希拉里一直用黑莓（BlackBerry）手机查看邮件。她申请了一部安全的政府配发黑莓手机。但她被告知不能在上面使用个人电子邮件账户。这是不允许的。而且不鼓励在政府电邮上处理个人事务。

00:09:50.790 --> 00:10:14.160
<v Garrett Graff>And if you remember, one of the first things Hillary Clinton said when this scandal broke in 2015 was, well, I didn't want to carry two devices. And everyone in Washington started laughing at that. Because most officials, most people in Washington, carry two or three devices.
<v Garrett Graff>如果你还记得，2015年这个丑闻曝光时，希拉里·克林顿说的第一件事就是，好吧，我不想携带两台设备。华盛顿的每个人都开始笑话这个说法。因为华盛顿的大多数官员，大多数人，都携带两到三台设备。

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:20.340
<v Sean Cole>Because there are strict prohibitions against using government phones or computers to do political campaigning. So multiple devices is normal.
<v Sean Cole>因为有严格的禁令禁止使用政府电话或电脑进行政治竞选活动。所以多台设备是常态。

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:50.090
<v Garrett Graff>But when we actually read through these documents, you can almost come away with a sense that that is actually why they did it, that Hillary seems to actually be a very persnickety technology user. She is not very comfortable with most technology, only liked to use a BlackBerry and, more specifically, only liked to use a specific type of increasingly older and more out-of-date BlackBerry.
<v Garrett Graff>但当我们通读这些文件时，你几乎可以得出结论，这确实是他们这么做的原因。希拉里似乎确实是一个非常挑剔的技术用户。她不太习惯大多数技术，只喜欢用黑莓手机，更具体地说，只喜欢用一种越来越老旧、过时的黑莓手机。

00:10:50.090 --> 00:10:52.690
<v Garrett Graff>She doesn't know how to use a desktop computer. She--
<v Garrett Graff>她不知道怎么用台式电脑。她……

00:10:52.690 --> 00:10:57.210
<v Sean Cole>Wait, wait, wait. Back up. Back up. She doesn't know how to use a desktop computer.
<v Sean Cole>等等，等等。倒回去。倒回去。她不知道怎么用台式电脑。

00:10:57.210 --> 00:11:11.000
<v Garrett Graff>Hillary Clinton, the Democratic presidential nominee in 2016, does not know how to use a desktop computer, according to multiple sources interviewed by the FBI.
<v Garrett Graff>据FBI采访的多个消息来源称，2016年民主党总统候选人希拉里·克林顿不知道如何使用台式电脑。

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:18.410
<v Sean    Cole>How is that possible?
<v Sean    Cole>这怎么可能？

00:11:18.410 --> 00:11:27.060
<v Garrett Graff>The short answer is, I don't know. I mean, I can get not knowing how to play an X-Box or a virtual reality machine or something like that.
<v Garrett Graff>简短的回答是，我不知道。我是说，我不懂怎么玩Xbox或者虚拟现实机之类的东西。

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:28.070
<v Sean Cole>Right.
<v Sean Cole>对。

00:11:28.070 --> 00:11:32.870
<v Garrett Graff>But a desktop computer, this is literally the oldest piece of personal technology available to us today.
<v Garrett Graff>但台式电脑，这真的是我们今天能用到的最古老的个人技术设备了。

00:11:32.870 --> 00:11:56.850
<v Sean  Cole>I looked into this. It's actually the abacus. At the same time, says Garrett, you have to think about where the Clintons were at the advent of the everyday internet. When Bill Clinton was elected in 1992, you could count the number of websites in the dozens. Bill himself didn't really properly use the internet until 1998. They just didn't live regular lives and never would again.
<v Sean  Cole>我研究过这个。其实是算盘。加勒特说，同时，你必须考虑克林顿夫妇在日常互联网出现时所处的阶段。1992年比尔·克林顿当选时，你可以用两位数数出网站的数量。比尔本人直到1998年才真正开始使用互联网。他们只是没有过上正常的生活，以后也永远不会了。

00:11:56.850 --> 00:12:19.290
<v Sean  Cole>When Hillary became Secretary of State, she asked one of her predecessors, Colin Powell, how he dealt with e-mail. He wrote to her, quote, "I didn't have a BlackBerry. What I did do was have a personal computer that was hooked up to a private phone line--" sounds ancient-- --"so I could communicate with a wide range of friends directly without it going through the State Department's servers. I even used it to do business with some foreign leaders."
<v Sean  Cole>当希拉里成为国务卿时，她问她的前任之一科林·鲍威尔（Colin Powell），他是怎么处理电子邮件的。他写信给她说：“我没有黑莓。我做的是有一台连接到私人电话线的个人电脑——”听起来很古老——“这样我就能直接与广泛的朋友交流，而不必经过国务院的服务器。我甚至用它与一些外国领导人做生意。”

00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:34.550
<v Sean  Cole>Powell advised her not to let the rules get too much in the way of communicating. But he also told her to be careful about using e-mail in general, as anything you write can become a matter of public record. He said all of this in an e-mail. He signed it, Love, Colin.
<v Sean  Cole>鲍威尔建议她不要让规则太阻碍交流。但他同时也告诉她要小心使用电子邮件，因为你写的任何东西都可能成为公共记录。他在一封邮件里说了这一切。他署名，爱你的，科林。

00:12:34.550 --> 00:13:05.200
<v Sean  Cole>Powell has also said he used a secure government computer for classified information. Anyway, his solution-- checking e-mail on a desktop computer-- wouldn't work for Hillary Clinton because, again, she didn't know how to operate one. So her staff embarked on a series of thrown-together work arounds. They kept buying her the antiquated type of BlackBerry she preferred, the Curve 8310 with the trackball. One staffer remembers having to buy one either on Amazon or eBay around 2013, after BlackBerry stopped making them.
<v Sean  Cole>鲍威尔也说过他用安全的政府电脑处理机密信息。总之，他的解决方案——在台式电脑上查看邮件——对希拉里·克林顿来说行不通，因为，再次强调，她不知道怎么操作电脑。所以她的员工着手进行了一系列拼凑的替代方案。他们一直给她买她喜欢的过时黑莓手机，带有轨迹球的Curve 8310。一位员工记得在2013年黑莓停止生产后，不得不通过亚马逊或eBay买了一部。

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:29.000
<v Sean  Cole>To complicate matters further, her office at the State Department was in a secure area where no BlackBerrys or cell phones were allowed. The worry was that spies could remotely turn them into listening devices. So the Secretary of State would leave her BlackBerry with a guard on the way in. And then, when she needed to check an e-mail, she'd get up from her desk, go get her phone, and wander around the eighth floor balcony.
<v Sean  Cole>更复杂的是，她在国务院的办公室位于一个安全的区域，那里禁止使用黑莓或手机。担心的是间谍可以远程将它们变成监听设备。所以国务卿在进入时会把她的黑莓留给警卫。然后，当她需要查看邮件时，她会从桌边站起来，去拿手机，然后在八楼的阳台上走来走去。

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:38.960
<v Sean  Cole>One more quick word about Colin Powell. He sometimes used a personal e-mail address to do State business. He used his AOL account.
<v Sean  Cole>关于科林·鲍威尔再多说一点。他有时用私人电子邮件地址处理国家公务。他用的是他的AOL账户。

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:48.940
<v Garrett Graff>And his argument, at the time, was a personal e-mail address is basically like a home telephone line. You can make personal calls on it. And you can make business calls on it.
<v Garrett Graff>他当时的理由是，私人电子邮件地址基本上就像一条家庭电话线。你可以用它打私人电话。也可以用它打公务电话。

00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:54.370
<v Sean Cole>So the Secretary of State using a private e-mail account wasn't anything new. It was frowned upon by the State Department, but it wasn't forbidden. And Garrett says, reading through the FBI files, using personal e-mail for government business was also rampant.
<v Sean Cole>所以国务卿使用私人电子邮箱并不是什么新鲜事。国务院对此不以为然，但并未禁止。加勒特说，阅读FBI的档案，使用个人邮箱处理政府业务也是普遍现象。

00:13:54.370 --> 00:14:25.250
<v Garrett Graff>State Department workers regularly, across the board, across all levels of the organization, regularly relied on personal e-mail in order to be able to conduct the business that the State Department needed to do, because they were traveling or because it was night time or it was a weekend or they were overseas away from an embassy.
<v Garrett Graff>国务院的工作人员定期，全线地，在组织的所有层级，都会依赖个人电子邮箱来处理国务院需要处理的业务，因为他们在外出旅行，或者是晚上，或者周末，或者是驻扎在海外远离大使馆。

00:14:25.250 --> 00:14:32.260
<v Sean Cole>Or for a much simpler and much more important reason-- the State Department computer network didn't work very well.
<v Sean Cole>或者是因为一个更简单、更重要的原因——国务院的计算机网络运行得不太好。

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:35.590
<v Garrett Graff>Part of what we learn in these FBI files is that that system actually wasn't compatible with the Wi-Fi that the Air Force uses--
<v Garrett Graff>我们在这些FBI文件中了解到的一部分是，该系统实际上与空军在飞机上使用的Wi-Fi不兼容——

00:14:35.590 --> 00:14:35.760
<v Sean Cole>Oh my god.
<v Sean Cole>哦，我的天哪。

00:14:35.760 --> 00:15:12.950
<v Garrett Graff>--on the planes that the Secretary of State travels on. So her staff would sometimes use private e-mails just when they were sitting on the plane with Hillary Clinton. People didn't think that the printer function worked very well within the State Department's regular e-mail system. So in order to print documents, they would often just forward documents to their personal Gmail or Hotmail, because it was easier to print out of that than it was to print out of the State Department e-mail system.
<v Garrett Graff>——在国务卿乘坐的飞机上。所以她的员工有时会直接在飞机上与希拉里·克林顿坐在一起时使用私人邮件。人们认为国务院常规邮件系统里的打印功能不太好用。所以为了打印文件，他们通常会把文件直接转发到自己的个人Gmail或Hotmail，因为从那里打印比从国务院邮件系统打印更容易。

00:15:12.950 --> 00:15:26.170
<v Sean Cole>This is one of the big, if accidental, revelations in this investigation-- just how crappy and backwards the State Department's computer system was. It's shocking for anyone who's ever worked in a normal business.
<v Sean Cole>这是本次调查中一个大的（如果说是偶然的）启示——国务院的计算机系统是多么糟糕和落后。对于任何在正常企业工作过的人来说，这都是令人震惊的。

00:15:26.170 --> 00:15:50.270
<v Garrett Graff>Moxt Americans would be absolutely horrified at the state of government technology writ large. But the State Department seems to be a particularly antiquated user of technology. When Colin Powell showed up at the State Department in 2001, he was given, in his office, a laptop that still relied on a 56K modem.
<v Garrett Graff>大多数美国人会对整个政府的技术水平感到绝对震惊。但国务院似乎是一个特别陈旧的技术用户。2001年科林·鲍威尔来到国务院时，他的办公室里配的是一台仍然依赖56K调制解调器的笔记本电脑。

00:15:50.270 --> 00:15:58.450
<v Sean Cole>If you're under 25, that was a way to dial into the internet over a phone line. And a phone line that used to-- it doesn't matter.
<v Sean Cole>如果你不到25岁，那是通过电话线拨号上网的一种方式。那条电话线以前……没关系。

00:15:58.450 --> 00:16:11.280
<v Garrett Graff>And Colin Powell made a really big effort to try to invest in actually putting computers on people's desks across the State Department, which did not exist in 2001.
<v Garrett Graff>科林·鲍威尔确实做出了很大的努力，试图投资在整个国务院员工的桌面上放置电脑，而这在2001年是不存在的。

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:11.440
<v Sean Cole>People didn't have computers on their desks in the State Department in 2001?
<v Sean Cole>2001年国务院员工桌上没有电脑？

00:16:11.440 --> 00:16:11.660
<v Garrett Graff>The average State Department employee at headquarters in Washington, DC in 2001 did not have a desktop computer.
<v Garrett Graff>2001年华盛顿特区国务院总部的普通员工没有台式电脑。

00:16:11.660 --> 00:16:32.030
<v Sean Cole>Or any kind of computer. No laptop. Nothing.
<v Sean Cole>或者任何类型的电脑。没有笔记本。什么都没有。

00:16:32.030 --> 00:17:03.310
<v Garrett  Graff>And Colin Powell was indeed shocked by that. So he bought 44,000 new computers. And when he would travel the world as Secretary of State, he would always sit down in embassies when he was visiting. He would find a computer, sit down, and check his own e-mail, in part as a test to see how well the organization and the embassies overseas were actually using the computers that he was giving them.
<v Garrett  Graff>科林·鲍威尔确实对此感到震惊。所以他买了44,000台新电脑。当他作为国务卿出访世界各地时，他总是会坐进大使馆。他会找一台电脑，坐下来，查看自己的邮件，部分是为了测试该组织和海外大使馆到底有多好地利用了他提供的这些电脑。

00:17:03.310 --> 00:17:33.080
<v Garrett  Graff>The shame of almost all of government is that government is, in many cases, the least sophisticated technology user in American society. You see this in our nuclear missile systems, where our nuclear ICBMs in their missile silos still rely on, like, floppy disks, which the average person in America hasn't used since they played Oregon Trail in elementary school.
<v Garrett  Graff>几乎所有政府的耻辱在于，政府在许多情况下是美国社会中技术最不精明的用户。你可以在我们的核导弹系统中看到这一点，我们的核洲际弹道导弹在发射井里仍然依赖类似软盘的东西，而美国普通人自小学玩《俄勒冈之旅》（Oregon Trail）以来就没再用过那东西了。

00:17:33.080 --> 00:17:33.610
<v Garrett  Graff>[LAUGHTER]
<v Garrett  Graff>[笑声]

00:17:33.610 --> 00:17:45.850
<v Sean   Cole>So Hillary's staff decided that it would be easiest for her to use her personal e-mail account for everything, the e-mail that was governed by her own private server.
<v Sean   Cole>所以希拉里的员工决定，让她用私人电子邮箱处理一切最方便，那个受她私人服务器管理的邮箱。

00:17:45.850 --> 00:18:08.080
<v Garrett Graff>It really just doesn't seem like anyone thought that deeply about this. Just, no one was really paying attention to the big picture. Collectively, Hillary Clinton's inner circle and the State Department leadership stumbled into this set up that very few people seem to have actually given any thought to the political implications of it.
<v Garrett Graff>真的好像没有人深入考虑过这个问题。只是，真的没有人关注大局。总的来说，希拉里·克林顿的核心圈子和国务院领导层跌跌撞撞地进入了这个设置，似乎很少有人真正考虑过它的政治含义。

00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:31.070
<v Sean Cole>Her aide, Huma Abedin, said she didn't recall any conversations about whether this was permissible. And as far as the rules for preserving e-mails under the federal laws, both Hillary and Huma told the FBI they figured any e-mails sent to an official State Department address by Hillary Clinton would be captured and preserved. They were later told, that's not how are you supposed to do it.
<v Sean Cole>她的助手胡玛·阿贝丁（Huma Abedin）说，她不记得有任何关于这样做是否允许的对话。至于根据联邦法律保存电子邮件的规则，希拉里和胡玛都告诉FBI，她们认为希拉里·克林顿发送到官方国务院地址的任何电子邮件都会被捕获并保存。她们后来被告知，这不是你应该做的方式。

00:18:31.070 --> 00:18:54.460
<v Sean Cole>The main accusation against Hillary, of course, is that she set up a private server to avoid scrutiny and cover her tracks. But what the FBI investigation found was that her aides were mostly uninterested in how or where her e-mails would be preserved. Huma Abedin, who herself had an account powered by the server, told the FBI she didn't know the server existed until after Hillary left the State Department.
<v Sean Cole>当然，对希拉里的主要指控是她设置了一个私人服务器来逃避审查并掩盖踪迹。但FBI的调查发现，她的助手对她的邮件如何或在哪里保存大多不感兴趣。胡玛·阿贝丁本人也有一个由该服务器支持的账户，她告诉FBI，直到希拉里离开国务院，她才知道这个服务器的存在。

00:18:54.460 --> 00:19:16.430
<v Sean Cole>Cheryl Mills, who was Hillary's chief of staff at the State Department, says she wasn't even sure she knew what a server was at that point. Which brings us to those two words you hear all the time in the coverage of E-mail-Gate-- private server. That sounds shady, right? The Clintons have a private e-mail server in their basement?
<v Sean Cole>谢丽尔·米尔斯（Cheryl Mills），当时希拉里的国务院幕僚长，说她甚至不确定当时是否知道什么是服务器。这把我们带到了你在电子邮件门报道中经常听到的那两个词——私人服务器。听起来很阴暗，对吧？克林顿夫妇地下室里有个私人电子邮件服务器？

00:19:16.430 --> 00:19:37.100
<v Sean Cole>Except what that means is that they had a computer that runs their e-mails, sends and receives and stores them. Lots of small businesses have an e-mail server. This American Life has one in a closet about 15 feet from where I'm sitting. And after Bill Clinton left the White House, he basically had a small office with a staff who needed e-mail accounts. So he had an e-mail server, an old Apple machine.
<v Sean Cole>除了这意味着他们有一台运行他们邮件、发送、接收并存储它们的计算机。很多小企业都有电子邮件服务器。我坐的地方15英尺外的壁橱里就有一台《美国生活》的服务器。比尔·克林顿离开白宫后，他基本上有一个带员工的小办公室，他们需要电子邮箱。所以他有一个电子邮件服务器，一台旧的苹果机器。

00:19:37.100 --> 00:20:09.210
<v Sean Cole>When you hear in the news that a server was installed in their basement just for Hillary, that's not true. A server was installed, but it was just an upgrade to the gear Bill Clinton already had for his staff. Less than a year into Hillary's tenure at the State Department, a couple of IT folks started to catch on that she was using a personal e-mail account. And there was some generalized brow-furrowing about making sure her staff was complying with the Federal Records Act, which requires all official communications be preserved.
<v Sean Cole>当你听到新闻里说地下室里装了一个专门给希拉里的服务器时，那不是真的。服务器是安装了，但那只是对比尔·克林顿已有员工装备的升级。希拉里在国务院任职不到一年，几个IT人员开始发现她在用个人电子邮箱。于是出现了一些关于确保她的员工遵守《联邦记录法》（要求保存所有官方通讯）的普遍焦虑。

00:20:09.210 --> 00:20:28.340
<v Sean Cole>And it was after Hillary left office in 2013 that the trouble really started. Some Russian and Ukrainian hackers found the server and tried to break into it. They didn't succeed. But Hillary's aides were spooked. And they scrambled to secure her e-mail. They decided to change her e-mail address. But then, they worried that they'd lose all the existing messages.
<v Sean Cole>是在希拉里2013年离职后，麻烦才真正开始。一些俄罗斯和乌克兰黑客发现了服务器并试图闯入。他们没有成功。但希拉里的助手们被吓坏了。他们匆忙保护她的邮件。他们决定更改她的电子邮箱地址。但随后，他们担心会丢失所有现有的信息。

00:20:28.340 --> 00:20:47.090
<v Sean Cole>It had happened in the past. When Hillary was a senator, she used an AT&T e-mail account. And AT&T didn't archive old e-mails. So whenever she got a new BlackBerry, she'd lose everything. So this time, again, it's 2013. It's up to her staff to save the e-mails, not a very complicated process.
<v Sean Cole>过去发生过。希拉里担任参议员时，用的是AT&T的邮箱。AT&T不会归档旧邮件。所以每次她拿到新黑莓手机，都会丢失所有内容。所以这一次，又是2013年。保存邮件取决于她的员工，这并不是一个很复杂的过程。

00:20:47.090 --> 00:21:08.920
<v Sean Cole>But rather than give it to an IT guy, an aide named Monica Hanley grabbed a laptop from Bill Clinton's Harlem office, brought it back to her apartment. And another staffer walked her through the steps of backing up the e-mails over the phone. She backed them up to a thumb drive too. After which, she lost the thumb drive.
<v Sean Cole>但与其交给IT人员，一名叫莫妮卡·汉利（Monica Hanley）的助手从比尔·克林顿的哈莱姆办公室拿了一台笔记本电脑，带回了自己的公寓。另一名员工通过电话指导她完成备份邮件的步骤。她还把邮件备份到了一个U盘里。之后，她把U盘弄丢了。

00:21:08.920 --> 00:21:35.290
<v Sean Cole>Then the server was moved out of Hillary Clinton's basement to a tech management company in New Jersey, called Platte River Networks, or PRN, as the guy who originally installed it and ran it wasn't working for Clinton anymore. Monica Hanley then tried to upload all of the e-mails she had transferred onto the laptop-- five years worth-- to the new PRN server, remotely from afar. She tried this a couple of times. But it kept not working.
<v Sean Cole>然后服务器从希拉里·克林顿的地下室搬到了新泽西州一家名为Platte River Networks（或PRN）的技术管理公司，因为最初安装和运行它的那个人不再为克林顿工作了。莫妮卡·汉利随后试图远程将她转移到笔记本电脑上的所有邮件——五年的量——上传到新的PRN服务器。她试了几次。但一直无法成功。

00:21:35.290 --> 00:21:44.050
<v Garrett Graff>So she ends up just FedEx-ing the whole MacBook to one of the IT staff. And then--
<v Garrett Graff>所以她最终只是把整个MacBook快递给了一位IT员工。然后——

00:21:44.050 --> 00:21:56.310
<v Sean Cole>Wait, wait, wait, wait, wait. There are e-mails on a laptop that potentially have State secrets within them that are being FedExed across the country?
<v Sean Cole>等等，等等，等等，等等。笔记本电脑上有潜在的国家机密邮件，正在全国范围内被快递？

00:21:56.310 --> 00:22:07.260
<v Garrett Graff>Yes. Although I don't think it seems clear that anyone involved actually thought that there were classified information inside of these e-mails.
<v Garrett Graff>是的。虽然我不认为有人认为参与其中的人真的认为这些邮件里包含机密信息。

00:22:07.260 --> 00:22:36.860
<v Sean Cole>But there was. At the end of 2014, the State Department made a formal request to former Secretary Clinton to hand over all of her work-related e-mails. The House Committee investigating the attack in Benghazi wanted to see them. Also, the State Department just didn't have any record of her communications while in office. Hillary and her staff handed over 30,000 e-mails, printed out, which amounted to 55,000 pages, 12 bankers boxes full of messages.
<v Sean Cole>但确实有。2014年底，国务院正式要求前国务卿克林顿交出所有与工作相关的电子邮件。调查班加西袭击事件的众议院委员会想看看它们。此外，国务院确实没有任何她任职期间的通讯记录。希拉里和她的员工交出了30,000封电子邮件，打印出来，总计55,000页，12箱满满的信息。

00:22:36.860 --> 00:22:53.020
<v Sean Cole>Her legal team determined which ones were work-related by using search terms, like .gov and names of Hillary's staffers. When they were done, they asked Hillary, do you want us to keep the personal e-mails? She replied, I have no use for them anymore. So they got rid of them.
<v Sean Cole>她的法律团队通过使用搜索词（如.gov和希拉里员工的名字）来确定哪些是工作相关的。完成后，他们问希拉里，你还要我们保留个人邮件吗？她回答，我不再需要它们了。所以他们就把它们处理掉了。

00:22:53.020 --> 00:23:30.520
<v Sean Cole>And this is where the narrative in the FBI interviews becomes like many other stories about Hillary Clinton, in that how you understand it depends on what you think of her. If you believe she's a sneaking, corner-cutting, conniver who does whatever she wants, this part of the story will totally convince you of that. After all, she destroys emails. But if you think she basically means well and is the focus of a seemingly endless witch hunt to pick apart every single thing she does, what she said about this in a press conference might seem entirely reasonable to you. Quote, No one wants their personal emails made public, and I think most people understand that and respect that privacy.
<v Sean Cole>这就是FBI访谈中的叙述变得像许多关于希拉里·克林顿的其他故事一样的地方，你如何理解它取决于你对她的看法。如果你认为她是个鬼鬼祟祟、投机取巧、随心所欲的策划者，这部分故事会完全说服你。毕竟，她销毁了邮件。但如果你认为她基本上是好意，并且是无穷无尽的针对她的一举一动进行拆解的猎巫行动的焦点，那么她在新闻发布会上说的这番话对你来说可能完全合理。引用：“没人愿意让自己的个人邮件公开，我认为大多数人理解并尊重这种隐私。”

00:23:30.520 --> 00:23:52.680
<v Sean Cole>Hillary Clinton's team ended up deleting 33,000 messages. You've probably heard this part. Then, the FBI resurrected about half of the deleted e-mails and discovered some of them were related to her time at the State Department. But FBI Director James Comey testified to Congress that the FBI, quote, "didn't find any evidence of evil intent and intent to obstruct justice."
<v Sean Cole>希拉里·克林顿的团队最终删除了33,000条信息。你可能听过这部分了。然后，FBI恢复了大约一半被删除的电子邮件，发现其中一些与她在国务院任职的时间有关。但FBI局长詹姆斯·科米向国会作证称，FBI“没有发现任何邪恶企图和妨碍司法的企图”。

00:23:52.680 --> 00:24:16.120
<v Sean Cole>In the e-mails that weren't deleted, there were 52 e-mail chains that contain information that was classified to some degree at the time there were sent. Eight of those e-mail chains contain information that was classified as Top Secret. By definition, we don't know what that information is or how damaging it might be to national security. But Garrett Graff says, a lot of times with our government, it's hard to pin down what the term "classified" even means.
<v Sean Cole>在未删除的电子邮件中，有52个邮件链包含在发送时在某种程度上属于机密的信息。其中8个邮件链包含被列为“绝密”的信息。根据定义，我们不知道这些信息是什么，也不知道它们对国家安全有多大的损害。但加勒特·格拉夫说，在我们的政府中，很难确定“机密”一词到底意味着什么。

00:24:16.120 --> 00:24:22.430
<v Garrett Graff>The average American, when they hear "classified information," thinks that that's a black-and-white term.
<v Garrett Graff>普通美国人听到“机密信息”，会认为那是一个黑白分明的词。

00:24:22.430 --> 00:24:22.470
<v Sean Cole>Right.
<v Sean Cole>对。

00:24:22.470 --> 00:24:22.600
<v Garrett Graff>That's binary. It is either classified, or it is not.
<v Garrett Graff>那是二元的。它要么是机密的，要么不是。

00:24:22.600 --> 00:24:22.640
<v Sean Cole>Right.
<v Sean Cole>对。

00:24:22.640 --> 00:24:48.700
<v Garrett Graff>And the government classification system is actually much messier than that. Different corners of the government may consider different things classified. Information on one day might be unclassified but might be classified on a later day, or vice versa.
<v Garrett Graff>而政府的分类系统实际上比这混乱得多。政府的不同部门可能认为不同的事物是机密的。同一天的信息可能是不机密的，但可能在后来的一天被归类为机密，反之亦然。

00:24:48.700 --> 00:24:51.420
<v Sean Cole>Like, retroactively classified, basically.
<v Sean Cole>就像，基本上是追溯性保密。

00:24:51.420 --> 00:24:55.400
<v Garrett Graff>Yeah, what in the business is called "up-classified."
<v Garrett Graff>是的，在业内被称为“上调密级”。

00:24:55.400 --> 00:25:21.940
<v Sean Cole>For instance, if something unclassified went to the National Security Council, someone there could decide to classify it. And that designation couldn't be changed back by someone downstream. And then, some information was classified, even though it's common knowledge. Under government secrecy law, Hillary and her top aides, discussing a New York Times article about the drone program-- that's considered a classified correspondence, because it's tacit admission that the drone program exists.
<v Sean Cole>例如，如果一些非机密信息发送到了国家安全委员会，那里的某个人可能会决定将其定为机密。而该指定不能由下游的人更改回来。此外，有些信息被归类为机密，尽管它们是常识。根据政府保密法，希拉里和她的高层助手讨论《纽约时报》关于无人机计划的一篇文章——这被认为是机密通信，因为它是对无人机计划存在的默许。

00:25:21.940 --> 00:25:24.680
<v Sean Cole>Which everybody knows. Because it was in the New York Times.
<v Sean Cole>每个人都知道这一点。因为那是《纽约时报》登的。

00:25:24.680 --> 00:25:43.260
<v Garrett Graff>There's this one e-mail-- probably the most famous e-mail of all of them-- involving this condolence call to the East African nation of Malawi, where the president had just died. And the Vice President had taken over. And Hillary Clinton was supposed to call.
<v Garrett Graff>有一封邮件——大概是所有邮件中最著名的一封——涉及对东非国家马拉维的吊唁电话，那里的总统刚去世。副总统接任了。希拉里·克林顿本应打电话。

00:25:43.260 --> 00:25:57.720
<v Garrett Graff>And it's this very innocuous e-mail. The line says, the purpose of call-- express condolences. And Hillary Clinton looks at that and says, I can't believe that that was actually classified. Like, that doesn't really make any sense to me.
<v Garrett Graff>这是一封非常无害的邮件。那行写着，通话目的——表达慰问。希拉里·克林顿看着它说，我真不敢相信那竟然被定为机密。那对我来说真的没有任何意义。

00:25:57.720 --> 00:26:20.600
<v Sean Cole>I've seen this e-mail. And it is confusing. It says "unclassified" at the top and a case number. And then, it says "classified" lower down. The innocuous part is marked with a C in parentheses, which means Confidential, the lowest of the three levels of classification the government uses. And then at the bottom, it says "unclassified" again.
<v Sean Cole>我见过这封邮件。它确实令人困惑。顶部写着“非机密”和一个案件编号。然后，下面写着“机密”。无害部分标有括号中的C，意思是机密（Confidential），政府使用的三个机密级别中的最低级。然后在底部，它再次写着“非机密”。

00:26:20.600 --> 00:26:39.010
<v Sean Cole>Hillary Clinton has said she didn't know that the c in parentheses meant the paragraph was classified. Again, what you make of that depends on how you see her. Either she's lying, or she just didnt know something basic about classified materials.
<v Sean Cole>希拉里·克林顿曾表示她不知道括号中的C意味着该段落是机密的。同样，你对此有何看法取决于你如何看待她。要么她在撒谎，要么她只是不知道关于机密材料的基本常识。

00:26:39.010 --> 00:27:09.450
<v Sean Cole>On Sunday, James Comey wrote to Congress saying the FBI had finished going through a whole slew of newly discovered emails. They were on a laptop that was confiscated in the investigation of Huma Abedin's husband, Anthony Wiener. Comey said nothing in the emails changed the conclusion that the FBI came to in July. That there was no criminal intent. But that Secretary Clinton and her colleagues, quote, were extremely careless in their handling of very sensitive, highly classified information.
<v Sean Cole>周日，詹姆斯·科米写信给国会，称FBI已经完成了对一大批新发现邮件的审查。这些邮件是在调查胡玛·阿贝丁的丈夫安东尼·韦纳（Anthony Weiner）时没收的一台笔记本电脑上发现的。科米说，邮件中没有任何内容改变了FBI在7月份得出的结论。即没有犯罪意图。但克林顿国务卿和她的同事们“在处理极其敏感、高度机密的信息时极其粗心”。

00:27:09.450 --> 00:27:27.850
<v Sean Cole>I should mention something Garrett Graff told me a few times. He's a reporter, he said. And somewhere in the darker part of his heart, he always kind of wants to uncover some sort of wrongdoing, some juicy conspiracy. And he's almost disappointed when he doesn't. But the times that he doesn't, those turn out to be all of the times.
<v Sean Cole>我应该提到加勒特·格拉夫几次告诉我的事。他说他是一名记者。在他内心的黑暗部分，他总是想揭露某种不法行为，某种有趣的阴谋。当他没有发现时，他几乎感到失望。但当他没有发现的时候，往往就是什么都没有的时候。

00:27:27.850 --> 00:27:54.930
<v Garrett Graff>Well, this scandal underscores to me so much of what I have experienced in covering politics and national security for a decade. Which is, whenever you hear a government conspiracy theory, the almost universal truth is that the explanation is more likely either bureaucratic bungling or outright incompetence.
<v Garrett Graff>嗯，这个丑闻对我来说太强调了我报道政治和国家安全十年来的经历。那就是，每当你听到政府阴谋论时，几乎普遍的真理是，解释更有可能是官僚主义的笨拙或彻头彻尾的无能。

00:27:54.930 --> 00:28:17.730
<v Sean   Cole>In a sense, this would be the easiest and most candid way Hillary Clinton could explain herself. Please forgive me. I and my staff, we just weren't that focused on my e-mails. We didn't care very much about that. We had other things to think about. Yeah, I guess it's not surprising she doesn't explain it that way.
<v Sean   Cole>从某种意义上说，这将是希拉里·克林顿最容易、最坦诚的解释方式。请原谅我。我和我的员工，我们只是没太关注我的邮件。我们不太关心那个。我们有其他事情要考虑。是的，我想她不那样解释并不奇怪。

00:28:17.730 --> 00:28:26.660
<v Ira  Glass>Sean Cole is one of the producers of our program.
