00:33:11.910 --> 00:33:24.600
<v Ira Glass>And with that in mind, we have arrived at Act Three of our show. Of Mice and Men. This story is from a live show that we did on stage in New York. It's from comedian Mike Birbiglia.
<v Ira Glass>考虑到这一点，我们进入了节目的第三幕。《人鼠之间》。这个故事来自我们在纽约舞台上做的现场演出。来自喜剧演员迈克·比比利亚（Mike Birbiglia）。

00:33:24.600 --> 00:33:46.190
<v Mike Birbiglia>I think that my favorite thing about being married is actually that you can share jokes with your wife or husband that are funny to you and that person and no one else other than, maybe, your cat. Because when you have a cat, your barometer for humor-- phh!-- out the window.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我想我结婚最喜欢的一点是，你可以和你的妻子或丈夫分享笑话，这些笑话对你和那个人来说很有趣，而对除你家猫以外的任何人来说都不好笑。因为当你养了猫时，你的幽默晴雨表——啪！——就飞出窗外了。

00:33:46.190 --> 00:34:13.340
<v Mike Birbiglia>Last summer, my wife and I went on a trip to Massachusetts. And I called it Catsachusetts, which is not funny but, in our house, was the joke of the year. I was like, we're going to Catsachusetts! My wife is like, ahh! I was like, ahh! Our cat was like, ahh!
<v Mike Birbiglia>去年夏天，我和妻子去马萨诸塞州旅行。我叫它“猫萨诸塞州”（Catsachusetts），这并不好笑，但在我们家，这是年度笑话。我说，我们要去猫萨诸塞州！我妻子说，啊！我说，啊！我们的猫说，啊！

00:34:13.340 --> 00:34:34.420
<v Mike Birbiglia>Everyone loves a good pun when you have a cat. And so, we drive the Catsachusetts. And when we arrive, my wife has a headache. And she asks me if I will acclimate Ivan-- that's our cat-- to the bedroom. Because you can't just put an indoor cat into a house, because he'll explode. And so, I bring in the bedroom.
<v Mike Birbiglia>当你养猫时，每个人都喜欢双关语。所以，我们开车去猫萨诸塞州。当我们到达时，我妻子头痛了。她问我是否能让伊万——那是我们的猫——适应卧室。因为你不能直接把室内猫放进房子，因为它会爆炸。所以，我把卧室安顿好。

00:34:34.420 --> 00:34:49.660
<v Mike Birbiglia>But I'm so tired from the drive that I fall asleep, which is the only thing you cannot do when acclimating an indoor cat to a house. And so, I wake up an hour later. Ivan is gone. He got out. And so, now I'm running around the house. I'm like, my cat's going to explode.
<v Mike Birbiglia>但我开车太累了，结果睡着了，这是让室内猫适应房子时唯一不能做的事情。所以，一小时后我醒来。伊万不见了。他跑出去了。所以，现在我在屋子里跑来跑去。我在想，我的猫要爆炸了。

00:34:49.660 --> 00:35:26.830
<v Mike Birbiglia>I wake up my wife. I say, Clo-- her name is Jen. I say, Mr. Fantastic is gone. His name's Ivan. And Clo gave me a look that I can only describe as divorce eyes. Because before that point, I was convinced that we would be married forever. And then, once I saw the divorce eyes, I was like, oh, I guess this could end. And if it ended, it would look a lot like that.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我叫醒我妻子。我说，克洛——她的名字叫詹——奇妙先生（Mr. Fantastic）不见了。他的名字叫伊万。克洛给了我一个我只能描述为“离婚眼神”的表情。因为在那之前，我一直确信我们会永远结婚。然后，一旦我看到了离婚眼神，我就想，哦，我想这可能会结束。如果它结束了，看起来会很像那样。

00:35:26.830 --> 00:35:40.970
<v Mike Birbiglia>And so, now the two of us are running around the house. I'm like, my marriage is falling apart! My cat's going to explode! And we find Ivan. But we had another major problem in the house, which is that there were mice in the house.
<v Mike Birbiglia>所以，现在我们俩在房子里跑来跑去。我在想，我的婚姻要崩溃了！我的猫要爆炸了！我们找到了伊万。但我们屋子里还有另一个大问题，那就是屋子里有老鼠。

00:35:40.970 --> 00:36:02.940
<v Mike Birbiglia>It was actually worse than that. Because they were parasitic mice. They have what's called toxoplasmosis, which means they have a-- yeah, you might know what this is. They have a parasite in them. And as a result, they're unafraid of cats. And they're unafraid of people.
<v Mike Birbiglia>实际上比那更糟。因为它们是寄生鼠。它们患有所谓的弓形虫病，这意味着它们有……是的，你可能知道这是什么。它们体内有一种寄生虫。结果，它们不怕猫。而且它们不怕人。

00:36:02.940 --> 00:36:33.500
<v Mike Birbiglia>And the way we discovered this was that my wife was watching TV. And she looked next to her. And there was a mouse. And he was watching TV also. And she sees screams! And he just looked up at her like Stuart Little, like, hey, what's going on? I don't like this show either. I don't know why all those women would want to marry that one guy.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我们发现这一点的方式是我妻子在看电视。她往旁边看去。有一只老鼠。他也正在看电视。她尖叫起来！他只是像《精灵鼠小弟》（Stuart Little）一样抬头看着她，嘿，怎么了？我也不喜欢这个节目。我不知道为什么那些女人会想嫁给那一个家伙。

00:36:33.500 --> 00:37:05.360
<v Mike Birbiglia>And then, she pushed him off the couch. And he didn't even run away. He didn't even scurry, which is a verb invented for mice. He just walked into the kitchen, like a roommate. Like, fine, I'll go in the other room. I just think you're overreacting. And then, he did a confessional into the mouse cam in the kitchen.
<v Mike Birbiglia>然后，她把他推下沙发。他甚至没有跑掉。他甚至没有“溜走”，这是专为老鼠创造的动词。他只是走进厨房，就像个室友。就像，好吧，我去别的房间。我只是觉得你反应过度了。然后，他在厨房的老鼠摄像机前进行了忏悔。

00:37:05.360 --> 00:37:15.960
<v Mike Birbiglia>He was like, I'm not here to make friends. I'm here to win. I was here before they came. I'll be here when they're gone. I'm a mouse.
<v Mike Birbiglia>他说，我来这里不是为了交朋友。我是为了赢。在他们来之前我就在这儿了。他们走了之后我也在这儿。我是一只老鼠。

00:37:15.960 --> 00:37:24.260
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声和掌声]

00:37:24.260 --> 00:37:51.450
<v Mike Birbiglia>That's The Real Mouse Lives of Catsachusetts. That night, I'm sound asleep. And my wife wakes me up by grabbing my face. She says, Mo-- my name's Mike-- Mr. Fantastic found the mouse. You need to get the mouse.
<v Mike Birbiglia>那是猫萨诸塞州的真实老鼠生活。那天晚上，我睡得正香。我妻子抓住我的脸把我叫醒。她说，莫——我的名字叫迈克——奇妙先生找到了老鼠。你需要去抓老鼠。

00:37:51.450 --> 00:38:22.960
<v Mike Birbiglia>And I sit up. And I say, Clo. We have a cat. We do everything for the cat. We give him food. We give him an apartment that he thinks is the world. We set aside an the area in the apartment for him to poop in that we clean more often than the area where we poop.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我坐起来。我说，克洛。我们有一只猫。我们为猫做一切。我们给他食物。我们给他一个他认为是全世界的公寓。我们在公寓里拨出一个区域让他排泄，我们打扫那个区域的频率比我们排泄的区域还要高。

00:38:22.960 --> 00:38:46.300
<v Mike Birbiglia>We have a gentleman's agreement that, in the unlikely event that a mouse should walk in that door, (MARLON BRANDO ACCENT) that he will kill that mouse. And we will never speak of that mouse again. And he will be protected.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我们达成了一个君子协定，万一哪天有老鼠走进了那扇门，（马龙·白兰度口音）他会杀了那只老鼠。我们永远不会再谈论那只老鼠。他会受到保护。

00:38:46.300 --> 00:38:57.170
<v Mike Birbiglia>(NORMAL VOICE) That's from Catfather.
<v Mike Birbiglia>（正常声音）那是来自《教父》。

00:38:57.170 --> 00:39:17.260
<v Mike Birbiglia>My wife says, Mo, get the mouse. And I sit up. And I see what may be the strangest tableau I will ever witness in my entire life. Ivan's smacking the mouse. The mouse flies in the air, lands, gets up, walks back towards Ivan.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我妻子说，莫，去抓老鼠。我坐起来。我看到了我一生中可能见过的最奇怪的场景。伊万在打老鼠。老鼠飞到空中，落地，站起来，走回伊万身边。

00:39:17.260 --> 00:39:50.140
<v Mike Birbiglia>Ivan smacks the mouse! It flies in the air, lands, gets up, walks back towards Ivan. Ivan is thrilled. His toy is alive! I have a serious sleepwalking disorder. So as I'm watching this, I'm not even really sure it's happening. I'm thinking, I've had this dream before.
<v Mike Birbiglia>伊万打了老鼠！它飞到空中，落地，站起来，走回伊万身边。伊万很兴奋。他的玩具活了！我有严重的梦游障碍。所以当我看着这一幕时，我甚至不确定这是不是真的发生了。我在想，我以前做过这个梦。

00:39:50.140 --> 00:40:34.970
<v Mike Birbiglia>My wife says, Mo, get the mouse. And she hands me a cup. I sit up. I walk towards the mouse. And the mouse walks towards me. I put the cup over the mouse. I put a magazine under the cup. I take the cup into the backyard. And I put the mouse into the forest, where I can only assume that he walked into the mouth of a wolf.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我妻子说，莫，去抓老鼠。她递给我一个杯子。我坐起来。我走向老鼠。老鼠走向我。我把杯子盖在老鼠身上。我在杯子底下放了一本杂志。我把杯子带到后院。我把老鼠放进了森林，我只能假设他走进了狼嘴里。

00:40:34.970 --> 00:40:39.200
<v Mike Birbiglia>And from that day forward, we have called it Mousachusetts.
<v Mike Birbiglia>从那天起，我们就叫它“鼠萨诸塞州”（Mousachusetts）。

00:40:39.200 --> 00:40:50.710
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声和掌声]

00:40:50.710 --> 00:41:44.000
<v Mike Birbiglia>I want to point out something really special that happened there at the end. A few minutes ago, I prefaced the story with a Massachusetts-based pun, Catsachusetts, which we all agreed, as a group, is not funny. Just moments ago, I concluded the story with another Massachusetts-based pun that was nearly identical. And that was Mousachusetts. And we applauded. Which means, in a way, it's like we're married.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我想指出最后发生的非常特别的事情。几分钟前，我以马萨诸塞州的双关语“猫萨诸塞州”为故事开场，我们大家一致认为，这不好笑。就在刚才，我以另一个几乎完全相同的马萨诸塞州双关语结束了故事。那就是“鼠萨诸塞州”。而且我们鼓掌了。这意味着，在某种程度上，就像我们结婚了一样。

00:41:44.000 --> 00:41:46.210
<v Mike Birbiglia>[LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[笑声和掌声]

00:41:46.210 --> 00:41:49.880
<v Ira  Glass>Mike Birbiglia--
<v Ira  Glass>迈克·比比利亚——

00:41:49.880 --> 00:41:53.550
<v Mike  Birbiglia>I do!
<v Mike  Birbiglia>我确实！

00:41:53.550 --> 00:42:17.500
<v Ira  Glass>His movie Don't Think Twice is available for pre-order on iTunes. It comes out there in a week.
<v Ira  Glass>他的电影《Don't Think Twice》可以在iTunes上预订。一周后就会在那里发布。

00:42:17.500 --> 00:42:22.500
<v Ira  Glass>[MUSIC -- "MICE!" FROM 3HREE, THE NEW MUSICAL]
<v Ira  Glass>[音乐 -- "MICE!" FROM 3HREE, THE NEW MUSICAL]

00:42:22.500 --> 00:42:36.060
<v Ira Glass>Squirrel Cop-- so we close our program today with this story from a police officer in a suburban community on the East Coast.
<v Ira Glass>松鼠警察——所以今天我们以东海岸一个郊区社区的一名警察的故事结束我们的节目。

00:42:36.060 --> 00:43:03.860
<v Police Officer>There was nothing, nothing going on. Saturday night in this village-- really quite, super cold. And this call came over for unknown animal in a house. And it was on my post. It was about five minutes away. So myself and another car were assigned the call. And we show up there. And luckily for me, it was another guy who was pretty new.
<v Police Officer>当时什么事都没有，什么事都没有。周六晚上在这个村庄——非常安静，超级冷。然后接到了一个关于屋里有不明动物的求助电话。就在我的管辖范围内。离那儿大约五分钟路程。所以我和另一辆车被分配到了那个电话。我们到了那里。幸运的是，那是另一个相当新的伙计。

00:43:03.860 --> 00:43:27.540
<v Police Officer>So we walk up to the door with all our stuff on, the nylon coat, the vest, the belt, the whole nine yards. And the door opens. And the guy who is behind the door-- he's about 30. I was 23 at the time. He's about 30. He looks like a broker or a lawyer. He's just really well put together, a nice guy, wearing glasses. He's wearing these, like, silk pajamas with a monogram. Got my attention.
<v Police Officer>所以我们穿着全套装备走上门，尼龙外套、背心、腰带，全部装备。门开了。那个站在门后的人——他大约30岁。我当时23岁。他大约30岁。他看起来像个经纪人或律师。他收拾得非常得体，是个好人，戴着眼镜。他穿着那种带有字母组合的丝绸睡衣。引起了我的注意。

00:43:27.540 --> 00:43:28.690
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:43:28.690 --> 00:43:46.770
<v Police Officer>And he's going, listen, I'm really sorry to bother you. Normally, I'd handle this sort of stuff on my own. But my wife really insists that I call. And so, we ask him what the problem is. He says, well, we were having kind of a romantic evening down in the living room. And we heard this scratching upstairs.
<v Police Officer>他说，听着，真的很抱歉打扰你们。通常，我会自己处理这种事情。但我妻子坚持让我打电话。所以，我们问他问题是什么。他说，嗯，我们在楼下客厅度过了一个浪漫的夜晚。然后我们听到楼上有抓挠声。

00:43:46.770 --> 00:43:59.330
<v Police Officer>So I ran upstairs to see what it was. And it turns out it's coming from the attic. There's something up there. And it's just running around knocking a few small things over. I can't tell what it is. It could be a squirrel, a raccoon. I really don't know.
<v Police Officer>所以我跑上楼去看那是什么。结果是从阁楼传出来的。上面有什么东西。它正在四处跑，撞翻了一些小东西。我看不出那是什么。可能是松鼠、浣熊。我真的不知道。

00:43:59.330 --> 00:44:16.700
<v Police Officer>So the other cop that I was with said, well, we really don't handle that. It's not so much a police function. But we do have numbers of these private contractors who will come in. And they'll put a humane trap down. And they'll remove the animal for you. And it's really not such a big deal. But it's really not our thing.
<v Police Officer>所以和我在一起的那个警察说，嗯，我们真的不处理那种事。这不是警察的职能。但我们确实有这些私人承包商的电话，他们会过来。他们会放一个陷阱。他们会为你移走动物。这真的没什么大不了的。但这真的不是我们的事。

00:44:16.700 --> 00:44:44.290
<v Police Officer>So right as he was in the middle of saying that and getting us off the hook, the guy swings the door back. And there's his wife, who was just beautiful. She was beautiful. She was probably about 26 or 27 but just really beautiful, like, perfect skin, long blond hair, great teeth, brilliant blue eyes, a really nice smile. Just like beautiful and friendly.
<v Police Officer>所以就在他正要说这话并帮我们摆脱困境时，那个男人把门推开了。那是他的妻子，真是太美了。她很美。她可能大约26或27岁，但真的非常漂亮，皮肤完美，金长发，牙齿洁白，明亮的蓝眼睛，非常甜美的微笑。就像美丽又友好。

00:44:44.290 --> 00:45:10.290
<v Police Officer>If she had said, eat this broken glass, I just would have said, OK, broken glass it is. That's fine. But she seemed really nice. So I was going to be, like, Galahad. So I just threw my arm back into this guy's chest, into my partner's chest, and I said, Mark, we can handle this.
<v Police Officer>如果她说，吃下这些碎玻璃，我只会说，好吧，碎玻璃就碎玻璃。没关系。但她看起来真的很好。所以我打算当那种加拉哈德（Galahad）式的人。所以我直接把手伸向那个伙计的胸口，伸向我搭档的胸口，我说，马克，我们可以处理这个。

00:45:10.290 --> 00:45:11.180
<v Police Officer>[LAUGHTER]
<v Police Officer>[笑声]

00:45:11.180 --> 00:45:22.810
<v Police Officer>It'll be OK. And she was just, thank you so much. And she was really sweet. And I was, like, struck dead. So we walk inside. And she goes, I'm going to throw a pot of coffee on. And we go upstairs. We follow the man of the house upstairs. And we're underneath one of those trapdoors that goes into the attic with the staircase that folds out.
<v Police Officer>会没事的。她只是说，太谢谢你了。她真的很好。我被彻底击中了。所以我们走进里面。她说，我去煮壶咖啡。我们上楼去。我们跟着那家的男主人上楼。我们来到阁楼门底下，那是那种有折叠楼梯的阁楼门。

00:45:22.810 --> 00:45:22.950
<v Ira Glass>Right.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:45:22.950 --> 00:45:47.530
<v Police Officer>And we do hear an animal upstairs, scratching away, just kind of scuttling around the floor. And there's definitely something up there. And it's making pretty good speed, going from one end of the roof to the other. So I reached up. And I took the trapdoor down. We unfolded the ladder.
<v Police Officer>我们的确听到楼上有动物，在抓挠，就在地板上爬来爬去。上面肯定有什么东西。它移动得很快，从屋顶这一端跑到那一端。所以我伸手。我把阁楼门拉下来。我们展开梯子。

00:45:47.530 --> 00:45:53.160
<v Police Officer>And I have this big heavy flashlight, like, your cop flashlight, the four D-cells, the metal case, the whole thing.
<v Police Officer>我拿着这把大又重的手电筒，就是那种警用手电筒，四个D号电池，金属壳，全套家伙。

00:45:53.160 --> 00:45:54.280
<v Ira Glass>Right.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:45:54.280 --> 00:46:14.300
<v Police Officer>And I shine it up through the hole in there. And it's pretty black. I can see the rafters, but really nothing else around there. And I start up the ladder. Now, the guy who owned the house is standing almost directly underneath me, just to the side of the ladder, looking straight up at me. And my partner is at the base of the ladder right behind me.
<v Police Officer>我透过那个洞把它照上去。上面很黑。我能看到椽子，但周围真的什么都看不到。我开始爬梯子。现在，那家房主几乎直挺挺地站在我下面，就在梯子旁边，直直地盯着我。我搭档在我后面，就在梯子底部。

00:46:14.300 --> 00:46:29.230
<v Police Officer>So just before I stuck my head through this black hole, I just kind of paused. Like, I crunched my body up underneath. Because I'm realizing, gee, you know, I don't know where this thing is. The second we pulled down the trapdoor, all noise upstairs just ceased. So I was kind of nervous.
<v Police Officer>所以就在我把头伸进这个黑洞之前，我停顿了一下。就像，我蜷缩着身体爬进去。因为我意识到，天哪，我不知道这东西在哪儿。我们拉下阁楼门的瞬间，楼上的所有噪音就都停止了。所以我有点紧张。

00:46:29.230 --> 00:46:54.460
<v Police Officer>And I was like, well, I look like an idiot just crouched up here on the top of the ladder. So I took the flashlight. And I just popped my head up, turned the light on again. And about six inches from the front of my face was this squirrel at eye level with me, kind of reared back on its legs. And I swear, from where I was standing, it looked like Godzilla.
<v Police Officer>我想，哎呀，我蜷在这里，梯子顶上，看起来像个白痴。所以我拿着手电筒。我只是把头伸上去，再次打开灯。离我的脸前方大约六英寸处，那只松鼠正与我平视，后腿站立。我发誓，从我站的角度看，它看起来就像哥斯拉。

00:46:54.460 --> 00:47:13.730
<v Police Officer>It just scared the heck out of me. I thought, it's a squirrel. It's going to be hiding somewhere. It's going to be terrified of me. It was six inches away from me. And it really startled me. So I kind of went, ah! and jumped back. And the flashlight slips out of my hands. And it's heavy. And it falls directly onto the nose of the guy who's looking straight up at me.
<v Police Officer>它真的吓坏我了。我想，这只松鼠。它会躲在某个地方。它会怕我。它离我只有六英寸。它真的吓到我了。所以我喊了一声，“啊！”并向后跳。手电筒从我手中滑落。它很重。它直接掉在了直直看着我的那个男人的鼻子上。

00:47:13.730 --> 00:47:21.630
<v Police Officer>And I don't think it broke it, but it did some damage. And his nose-- his hands up to his face. Blood just started pouring out between his hands.
<v Police Officer>我不认为它打断了鼻子，但造成了一些损伤。他的鼻子……他的手捂住脸。鲜血开始从他的指缝间涌出。

00:47:21.630 --> 00:47:23.530
<v Ira Glass>This is the homeowner?
<v Ira Glass>这是房主？

00:47:23.530 --> 00:47:39.500
<v Police Officer>This is the homeowner. I lose my balance and fall backwards, directly onto my partner. And I just pancake him. We're both on our backs. He's on his back. I'm on his stomach on my back, scuttling around like a beetle, trying to get up in this really narrow hallway. It's a mess.
<v Police Officer>这是房主。我失去平衡向后倒，直接压在我搭档身上。我像煎饼一样把他压扁了。我们都躺在背上。他仰面朝天。我在他肚子上，背朝下，像只甲壳虫一样四处爬动，试图在这个非常狭窄的走廊里站起来。真是团糟。

00:47:39.500 --> 00:47:49.860
<v Police Officer>The squirrel, while we're floundering around in the hallway, jumps down the stairs-- boink, boink, boink-- lands on me and takes off down the stairs.
<v Police Officer>当我们在这走廊里挣扎时，松鼠跳下楼梯——咚、咚、咚——落在我身上，然后朝楼下跑去。

00:47:49.860 --> 00:47:52.050
<v Ira Glass>How undignified!
<v Ira Glass>多么毫无尊严！

00:47:52.050 --> 00:48:09.340
<v Police Officer>It was terrible. It was terrible. So we're wondering, gee, where's the squirrel? An right at that second, the woman who lived there-- you hear her scream. So my partner goes, well, we found the squirrel. It's wherever she is.
<v Police Officer>太糟糕了。太糟糕了。所以我们想，哎呀，松鼠在哪儿？就在那一刻，住在那里的那个女人——你听到她尖叫了。所以我的搭档说，嗯，我们找到松鼠了。它在哪儿她就在哪儿。

00:48:09.340 --> 00:48:10.840
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:48:10.840 --> 00:48:37.060
<v Police Officer>So we go running downstairs. And the squirrel had come into the living room where they had been having their romantic evening. They had a fire going. They had pillows arranged around one corner of the couch next to the fire. And they had champagne flutes out and-- nice house, really nice. I mean, it just smelled brand new, new carpeting, new rugs, new paint. They hadn't been there for that long.
<v Police Officer>所以我们跑下楼。松鼠进了客厅，他们刚才还在那里度过浪漫夜晚。他们生了火。他们在火堆旁边的沙发角落里布置了枕头。他们拿出了香槟杯，装修很棒，真的很好。我的意思是，闻起来就像崭新的，新的地毯，新的挂毯，新的油漆。他们搬来没多久。

00:48:37.060 --> 00:48:59.440
<v Police Officer>So the squirrel, when it bolted down the staircase, took off into the living room and ran underneath the couch for cover. So we run downstairs. This guy is bleeding all over the place on his carpets. His wife looks and says, what have you done to my husband? And I start going, oh, it was an accident. And I just stop in mid-sentence. What's the point? We've only been there about two minutes.
<v Police Officer>所以松鼠，当它冲下楼梯时，跑进客厅，躲在沙发下面。我们跑下楼。这家伙的血到处流在他地毯上。他妻子看着说，你对我丈夫做了什么？我开始说，哦，那是个意外。然后我在半句话中停住了。有什么意义呢？我们才在那儿待了两分钟。

00:48:59.440 --> 00:49:19.180
<v Police Officer>So the squirrel is underneath the couch. And my partner is going, you know, let's get out of here. This is just not going well. So I-- I'm not beaten yet. I always have another idea. So the squirrel is under this couch, which is in the middle of the room. So I have this bright idea.
<v Police Officer>所以松鼠在沙发下。我的搭档说，你知道，我们离开这儿吧。这进展得一点都不顺利。所以我——我还没被打败呢。我总有另一个主意。所以松鼠在沙发下，沙发在房间中间。所以我有个好主意。

00:49:19.180 --> 00:49:33.750
<v Police Officer>Why don't we move the furniture away from one of the corners and we'll put the couch in the corner. And the squirrel will probably move along with the couch, because it's the only cover available to it. And once we get it into the corner, we'll only have two open sides of the couch to worry about. So we did that.
<v Police Officer>我们把家具从一个角落移开，然后把沙发放到那个角落。松鼠很可能会跟着沙发移动，因为那是它唯一能用的遮蔽物。一旦我们把它赶进角落，我们就只需要担心沙发的两个敞开面了。所以我们就这么做了。

00:49:33.750 --> 00:49:35.740
<v Ira Glass>That is so tactical!
<v Ira Glass>那太有战术了！

00:49:35.740 --> 00:49:54.050
<v Police Officer>Yes. Yeah, I was very proud of myself at that instant. But you know, I asked her for a box. And she says, sure, we've got boxes. We just moved in. We have nothing but boxes. She runs out to the garage. And she comes back with a box. And the box is long enough and it fits across the entire short side of the couch, where the armrest would be.
<v Police Officer>是的。是的，那一刻我为自己感到非常自豪。但是，你知道，我问她要个盒子。她说，当然，我们有盒子。我们刚搬进来。我们除了盒子什么都没有。她跑向车库。她带回一个盒子。盒子足够长，刚好横跨沙发较短的那一边，就是扶手所在的位置。

00:49:54.050 --> 00:50:10.070
<v Police Officer>So I start sweeping underneath the couch with my nightstick, trying to move the squirrel toward the box, figuring we'll capture it and just get rid of it. And we'll be out here. And there'll be no more mayhem. So it's actually working very well. And the squirrels moving down along.
<v Police Officer>所以我开始用我的警棍在沙发下扫，试图把松鼠朝盒子那边赶，心想我们抓到它就把它弄走。我们就出去了。就不会再有骚乱了。所以它实际上进展得很好。松鼠正在移动。

00:50:10.070 --> 00:50:10.440
<v Police Officer>You can hear it. It's chittering. And I'm trying not to hurt it. I feel kind of-- I'm nervous about the thing. It might bite me. I don't want to hurt it really. It's just an animal.
<v Police Officer>你能听到它。它在尖叫。我尽量不去伤害它。我感觉……我对那东西感到紧张。它可能会咬我。我真的不想伤害它。它只是一只动物。

00:50:10.440 --> 00:50:10.480
<v Ira Glass>Right.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:50:10.480 --> 00:50:38.000
<v Police Officer>So I'm moving it along. And everything is going very well. And then, with about eight inches to go, I took one more swipe. And the thing just bolted out from underneath the couch. It was lined with, like, tassels. I couldn't really see under the couch. It bolted out from underneath the couch and ran directly into the fireplace, which is about three feet away.
<v Police Officer>所以我把它赶走。一切都进展得很顺利。然后，只剩下八英寸了，我又扫了一下。那东西直接从沙发下面冲了出来。沙发下面镶着流苏之类的东西。我真的看不见沙发下面。它直接从沙发下冲出来，直接跑进了火炉里，大约三英尺远。

00:50:38.000 --> 00:50:40.850
<v Police Officer>The fireplace was directly ahead of it. And it ran into the fire--
<v Police Officer>火炉就在它正前方。它跑进火里——

00:50:40.850 --> 00:50:40.900
<v Ira Glass>Oh my!
<v Ira Glass>哦，天哪！

00:50:40.900 --> 00:51:14.790
<v Police Officer>--and caught on fire and ran directly back out and directly back under the couch. (INTERVIEWER) IRA GLASS: What? Is it on fire? (SUBJECT) POLICE OFFICER: It was on fire. Yeah, the tail, the bushy fur, the whole bit. It wasn't flaming or anything. But it was smoking. And there was a little bit of fire coming off the tail. So it runs back under the couch. And the couch catches on fire in seconds, I mean, in seconds. It must have had dust under there or something else. But it just caught on fire immediately.
<v Police Officer>——着火了，然后直接跑出来，又钻回了沙发底下。（采访者艾拉·格拉斯）什么？它着火了？（受访者警察）它着火了。是的，尾巴，毛茸茸的毛，全部都着了。虽然没有火焰之类的。但它在冒烟。而且尾巴上确实有点火。所以它跑回沙发底下。沙发几秒钟内就着火了，我的意思是，几秒钟内。沙发下面一定有灰尘或别的什么东西。但它确实立刻着火了。

00:51:14.790 --> 00:51:43.790
<v Police Officer>And my partner and I just don't even talk. We just grab the couch, heave it upside down. And now there's plenty of oxygen for the fire to really get going. And it starts up. And we're patting it out. And it's sort of getting away from us. So we grab the only thing that's really available. And those are these really nice silk pillows. And we have one in each hand, the both of us, and we're just windmilling away at this fire on the couch. And we put it out. But it's smoking terribly.
<v Police Officer>我和我的搭档连话都不说。我们只是抓住沙发，把它掀翻。现在有充足的氧气供火势蔓延。火着起来了。我们拍打着火苗。火有点失控了。所以我们抓起唯一能用的东西。那就是那些漂亮的丝绸枕头。我们每个人手里拿一个，我们对着沙发上的火一阵狂挥。我们把火扑灭了。但沙发在冒着可怕的烟。

00:51:43.790 --> 00:52:10.980
<v Police Officer>And it was just-- it was a disaster. The couch is upside down. The bottom of it is burnt. The house is filling with smoke from the couch. The squirrel, when it went under the couch in its death throes, just latched onto the bottom of the couch. It's like this smoking piece of gristle underneath the couch, latched on there with its claws. And were pounding, smearing it all over the place. And the smoke alarms are firing away.
<v Police Officer>这简直是一场灾难。沙发翻倒了。底部被烧焦了。房子里塞满了来自沙发的烟。松鼠，当它在垂死挣扎中钻进沙发下时，就死死抓在沙发底部。它像一块冒烟的软骨一样抓在沙发下面，爪子紧紧扣住。我们拍打着，把它 smeared 到处都是。烟雾警报器响个不停。

00:52:10.980 --> 00:52:34.950
<v Police Officer>The guy is standing with handkerchiefs and paper towels up around his nose, which is still bleeding. His pajamas are a mess. They're covered with blood, the front of them. And we finally get the fire out. And we're both completely red, sweating. Because we're dressed for, like, zero degree weather. And it's hot there by the fire. We're mortified. The house is full of smoke.
<v Police Officer>那家伙拿着手帕和纸巾捂着鼻子，鼻子还在流血。他的睡衣一团糟。上面沾满了血，前襟都是。我们终于把火扑灭了。我们俩完全满脸通红，大汗淋漓。因为我们穿的是零度天气的衣服。而且火边很热。我们感到非常尴尬。房子里全是烟。

00:52:34.950 --> 00:52:55.560
<v Police Officer>The wife just looks around and just starts to cry. She goes, what-- what have you done? What have you done to my house? You could see her just clicking things off on her fingers. OK, dead squirrel, ruined pillows, need a new couch. The walls are covered with soot. The fire alarms are going off. My husband's disfigured. And then, she really kind of just lost it.
<v Police Officer>妻子只是环顾四周，开始哭了起来。她说，你……你们做了什么？你们对我的房子做了什么？你可以看到她在手指上数着东西。好吧，死松鼠，毁了的枕头，需要买个新沙发。墙壁覆盖着煤烟。火警响个不停。我丈夫毁容了。然后，她真的有点崩溃了。

00:52:55.560 --> 00:53:26.190
<v Police Officer>And he was just looking at us and shaking his head like he couldn't believe that these two idiots showed up and did this to his house over nothing really. And he just goes, you know, you really haven't done anything wrong. I can't point to any one thing that you did that I have a reason to get angry about. You really haven't done anything wrong. I mean, we did call you. But I just-- I can't thank you for this.
<v Police Officer>他只是看着我们，摇着头，好像不相信这两个白痴跑来，为了一点点小事就把他的房子弄成这样。他说，你知道，你们真的没做错什么。我指不出你们做的哪件事我有理由生气。你们真的没做错什么。我的意思是，我们确实叫了你们。但我就是……我无法为此感谢你们。

00:53:26.190 --> 00:53:49.770
<v Police Officer>They call for a squirrel. They end up with, like, $3,000, $4,000 worth of damage and a broken nose. And this is all within about five minutes. As it turned out, the squirrel-- it was a Pyrrhic victory for the squirrel. But the squirrel definitely won. The squirrel really kicked our ass.
<v Police Officer>他们为了松鼠打电话。最后造成了大约三四千美元的损失和一个断鼻子。这一切都在五分钟内发生。事实证明，那只松鼠——对松鼠来说那是皮洛士式的胜利。但那只松鼠绝对赢了。那只松鼠真的把我们打得落花流水。

00:53:49.770 --> 00:53:55.410
<v Ira Glass>(LAUGHING) That is not what you want to be saying at the end of the day.
<v Ira Glass>（大笑）这可不是你希望在一天结束时说的话。

00:53:55.410 --> 00:53:56.900
<v Police Officer>No, no.
<v Police Officer>不，不。

00:53:56.900 --> 00:54:01.880
<v Ira Glass>Could that have happened to you now 13 years later?
<v Ira Glass>13年后这种情况还会发生在你身上吗？

00:54:01.880 --> 00:54:23.860
<v Police Officer>There's always a new mistake to be made. I don't think I would make that particular mistake. I mean, you make plenty of mistakes. You make plenty of mistakes. That's just part of that job. You just try not to make the same one twice. But there's such variety that, you're going to make hundreds-- you're going to make thousands of mistakes. You're going to make thousands of mistakes until you really get a handle on what you're doing.
<v Police Officer>总有新的错误可以犯。我不认为我会再犯那同一个特定的错误。我的意思是，你会犯很多错误。你会犯很多错误。这是那份工作的一部分。你只是尽量不要犯同一个错误两次。但有太多种类的错误了，你会犯数百个……你会犯数千个错误。在你真正掌握你在做什么之前，你会犯数千个错误。

00:54:23.860 --> 00:54:54.220
<v Ira Glass>When that interview first ran on our program a long time ago, our interviewee, who asked not to be named on the radio, has now been on the force for 31 years.
<v Ira Glass>当我们很久以前在节目中第一次播出那次采访时，那位要求不在广播中透露姓名的受访者，现在已经在警队工作了31年。

